WS73 bounded C verification
===========================

The separate USBStop.README describes the finite TLC stop-producer lifetime
model and its historical-order counterexample. Its abstraction and evidence
are independent of the production-C scalar checks described here.

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/formal/run.py

Requires CBMC and a C preprocessor. The runner reserves the WS73 build lock,
uses one solver process at a time, limits each invocation to 60 seconds and
4 GiB address space, and records every attempt. It performs symbolic analysis
on the host; it does not execute/install a kernel, driver or firmware there.
Check other build/resource use before starting. Inputs must be committed and
clean; inputs, tool candidates, commands, raw results and hashes are archived
under ../.dev/ws73-upstream/records/cbmc-hcc-<timestamp>.

hcc.c includes the actual production drivers/misc/ws73/hcc.c translation unit
and include/linux/ws73.h types. Entry points verify chip/version query encoding
and chip/version reply decoding for CBMC's i386 and x86_64 data models. Other
functions in that production file are not covered by these entry points.

Input byte values, length/capacity over the entire size_t domain, null-input
and null-output alternatives, and initial output values are symbolic. Caller
objects are valid, retained and non-overlapping. Input starts at an unaligned
offset. Allocation is exactly min(declared length, inspected prefix)+1 for
a one-byte leading guard: 18/24-byte reply prefixes or a 32-byte query output.
For short declarations, an overread/overwrite crosses the allocation boundary;
for longer declarations, this deliberately smaller object additionally checks
that the converter does not access the uninspected suffix. An arbitrary index
checks input preservation and failure-output preservation over that prefix.
Reply output fields are checked directly, not through padding comparisons.

Assertions check exact acceptance predicates, query bytes, error-output
immutability and preservation of successful identity/version values. CBMC
also checks pointers, bounds, pointer arithmetic, signed/unsigned overflow,
conversion, division and allocation leaks. Loop unwind is 33, including an
unwinding assertion for the 32-byte query comparison; incomplete unwinding
cannot produce a positive result. Null allocated objects are excluded as the
caller's valid-storage precondition; protocol NULL arguments are explored.

The shim models only kernel scalar types, errno constants, module metadata,
memset declarations and bytewise little-endian unaligned helpers. Kernel
helper implementation equivalence, the CBMC frontend/solver/library models,
preprocessor and retained-object assumptions form the trusted base. Scalar
width assertions guard the type model. No SDK source or firmware is included.

Five private negative controls mutate copies of production code: wrong query
tag, wrong query role byte, partial chip-output publication, omitted record
length bound, and wrong returned ROM value. Each must produce its specific
counterexample; unrelated failures, warnings, missing properties, unknown
results, timeouts and abnormal exits are not accepted. These copies are never
compiled into a kernel or committed as production changes.
Before each solver invocation, the runner obtains --show-properties with the
same model/check options. Every result ID and description must match that
complete inventory, including memory checks, as well as the explicit contract
set. A success banner or a remaining subset of assertions is insufficient.

This is conditional bounded C verification of four scalar converters, not
firmware authenticity/compatibility, boot register reliability, USB aggregation,
H4 streaming, concurrent lifetime/refinement, TLC/LKMM, liveness or hardware
qualification. Passing these models does not close the KCSAN baseline reports
or permit firmware loading on an unqualified physical device.
